What is the distance between the point $A=(1,-2,6)$ and the line given by the parametric equation $\dfrac{x-5}{1}=\dfrac{y-6}{3}=\dfrac{z-5}{-4k}$ for $k \in \mathbb{R}$?

The distance from $A$ to the line can be interpreted as the length of the orthogonal projection of the line segment $\overrightarrow{PA}$ onto the plane containing point $A$ perpendicular to the given line.

 First we compute a vector orthogonal to the line.  Using the line parametrization, we can express $x-5 = t$, $y-6 = 3t$, and $z-5 = -4kt$.  This means
\[(x-5)\mathbf{i} + (y-6)\mathbf{j} + (z-5)\mathbf{k} = t\mathbf{i} + 3t\mathbf{j} - 4kt\mathbf{k}.\][asy]
arrow((5,O),(6,O),E); arrow((5,O),(6,O),W);
arrow((0,-2,O),(0,-2,O),W); arrow((0,-2,O),(5,-2,O),W);
arrow((0,-2,O),(0,-2,O),N); arrow((0,6,O),(0,6,O),N);
arrow((O,6,O)*(O,6,O),S);
